2026-10-22T04:55:00Z  NOTE     The rights call works with NO credential at all: an anonymous request is answered with a guest token, and the key unwraps under SHA-256 of that token. The client sends the bearer because it has one and the site's own client does the same, but the fallback exists and is what the capture used.
2026-10-22T13:35:00Z  CHANGE   SunoDownloader::download(): the server's `is_download_unlocked` flag is no longer a veto. It refused 36 of the 40 captured clips before a byte was fetched, which is why every play button was dead. The flag governs Suno's DOWNLOAD PRODUCT (`/api/download/clip/{id}?format=mp3`, the meter the site's Download button and its "download credits" upsell are about), which this client does not call; what it fetches is the progressive asset the account already streams, unsigned, from the captured CloudFront host, decrypted locally with the clip's own content key. The lock is still read and logged (`downloadProductLock()`), and what refuses now is the server itself: a 403 from the media host or a 401 from the rights route.
2026-10-22T13:35:00Z  CHANGE   SunoDownloader: content keys are cached per clip for one hour (kContentKeyTtl), mirroring the site's own license client (`CONTENT_KEY_CACHE_TIME = 3600000`, keyed content_id:content_type, reporting cache-vs-network). A replay or a re-save spends no `/api/mango/rights` call. Only a key that authenticated is cached; a refusal is never stored.
2026-10-22T13:35:00Z  CHANGE   tests/unit/suno/test_DownloadEntitlement.cpp: rewritten around the new semantics -- a locked clip with and without a reason now DOWNLOADS (asserted as a real request plus a saved file, so the veto cannot return quietly); the lock notice is swept for quota/credit overclaims; the format, unfinished-status and already-on-disk branches still refuse/reuse exactly as before. tests/unit/suno/test_SunoDownloader.cpp: aContentKeyIsReusedWithinTheHour pins the cache with two transfers of one clip and one key request.
2026-10-22T13:35:00Z  VERIFY   Live route probes (anonymous, one clip id, 2026-10-22): POST https://studio-api-prod.suno.com/api/mango/rights -> 200 with key/iv/glt; POST https://studio-api.prod.suno.com/api/mango/rights -> 200; POST https://suno.com/api/mango/rights -> 404 (a Next.js error page, not an API). The site's own 2026-10-16 bundle configures its service worker with `sendApiSetup(..., "https://studio-api-prod.suno.com")` and the worker fetches `${apiBase}/api/mango/rights`, so the claim that the route had moved to suno.com/api is refuted by both the site and the live server.
2026-10-22T13:35:00Z  NOTE     Suno's browser-only shortcut, recorded so nobody wires it: `/_sw-mango/passthrough` and `/_sw-mango/manifest.m3u8` return DECRYPTED bytes, but they are service-worker routes -- they exist only for pages the worker controls, so a native client cannot call them. The worker's own two steps are `POST /api/mango/rights` then AES-128-CTR, which is what this client implements. Two current third-party downloaders (Mikesco3/suno-ai-downloader, RanaUniverse/suno_song_download_backend) use those routes via Chromium or reimplement exactly this chain; neither shows a cheaper route, and the second is byte-for-byte the algorithm here (SHA-256(glt) -> AES-GCM -> AES-128-CTR -> .m4a).
